WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.362
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.662 --> 00:00:09.292
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:16.540
Unit six: Colonial History. Today you will describe long-lasting effects of the past with the present perfect.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.353
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.289
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.302
Colonialism.

7
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:31.184
Colonialism means the control of one country by another, often for economic gain.

8
00:00:31.783 --> 00:00:34.433
Colonialism has shaped many modern borders.

9
00:00:35.017 --> 00:00:38.064
Your turn. Say: Colonialism.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.087
Colonialism.

11
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:43.793
Empire.

12
00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:47.967
Empire means a group of territories ruled by one power.

13
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:51.129
The French empire included Indochina.

14
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.206
Your turn. Say: Empire.

15
00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:58.021
Empire.

16
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:00.156
Exploitation.

17
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.216
Exploitation means unfair use of people or resources for profit.

18
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:09.337
Rubber plantations relied on the exploitation of labour.

19
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.451
Your turn. Say: Exploitation.

20
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.673
Exploitation.

21
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:18.487
Resistance.

22
00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:23.050
Resistance means opposition to occupation or control.

23
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.391
Local resistance grew throughout the 1940s.

24
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:31.251
Your turn. Say: Resistance.

25
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.310
Resistance.

26
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.248
Independence.

27
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.387
Independence means freedom from control by another country.

28
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:46.026
Cambodia gained independence in 1953.

29
00:01:46.617 --> 00:01:49.141
Your turn. Say: Independence.

30
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:53.233
Independence.

31
00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:55.066
Legacy.

32
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.990
Legacy means something that remains from the past.

33
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:02.372
The legal system is a colonial legacy.

34
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:05.578
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

35
00:02:08.433 --> 00:02:09.295
Legacy.

36
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.295
Indigenous.

37
00:02:12.283 --> 00:02:15.943
Indigenous means originating naturally in a particular place.

38
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.571
Indigenous communities lost access to their land.

39
00:02:20.183 --> 00:02:23.010
Your turn. Say: Indigenous.

40
00:02:25.867 --> 00:02:27.007
Indigenous.

41
00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.809
Sovereignty.

42
00:02:29.817 --> 00:02:32.850
Sovereignty means a nation's right to govern itself.

43
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.251
Independence restored Cambodia's sovereignty.

44
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:39.224
Your turn. Say: Sovereignty.

45
00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:43.242
Sovereignty.

46
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:47.591
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:55.619
Visal, Cambodia became a French protectorate in 1863. Why does that still matter today?

48
00:02:56.717 --> 00:03:07.008
Because colonialism has shaped many modern structures. The borders with Vietnam and Thailand have been influenced by maps drawn under French rule.

49
00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:10.430
What other legacies can you identify?

50
00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:22.598
The legal system, some architecture in Phnom Penh, and French loanwords like 'kafe'. These have remained part of daily life for generations.

51
00:03:23.683 --> 00:03:25.845
Was the period entirely negative?

52
00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:38.287
The exploitation was real, especially on the rubber plantations. Historians have debated other effects, but independence clearly restored our sovereignty.

53
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:40.748
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:45.406
Grammar focus: Present perfect for lasting effects.

55
00:03:45.706 --> 00:03:46.916
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:47.617 --> 00:03:50.348
Colonialism has shaped many modern borders.

57
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.876
French loanwords have remained part of daily life.

58
00:03:54.783 --> 00:03:57.630
Historians have debated its effects for decades.

59
00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:12.507
Use the present perfect to connect a past cause with a present result: 'Colonialism has shaped borders' (they still exist). Use the past simple for finished events with a date: 'France colonised Cambodia in 1863.'

60
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:18.526
Be careful! Don't say: Cambodia has gained independence in 1953.

61
00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:25.485
Say: Cambodia gained independence in 1953.

62
00:04:25.785 --> 00:04:27.796
Use past simple with a finished date.

63
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.531
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.333 --> 00:04:34.066
The Long Shadow of Empire.

65
00:04:34.366 --> 00:04:40.232
Between the sixteenth and twentieth centuries, European empires extended their control across much of the world.

66
00:04:41.233 --> 00:04:48.097
In Southeast Asia, France established Indochina, while Britain and the Netherlands ruled neighbouring territories.

67
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:57.996
Although most colonies gained independence after 1945, colonialism has left lasting legacies in borders, languages and economies.

68
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:05.179
Many former colonies have struggled with institutions designed to extract resources rather than to serve citizens.

69
00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:12.775
Historians have increasingly focused on the voices of the colonised, including indigenous peoples and resistance movements.

70
00:05:13.867 --> 00:05:17.717
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.583 --> 00:05:22.510
The colonial legal system blank influenced modern law.

72
00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.162
A, has. B, have. C, was.

73
00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.723
The answer is A: has.

74
00:05:32.883 --> 00:05:36.845
Cambodia blank independence in 1953.

75
00:05:37.145 --> 00:05:40.334
A, has gained. B, gained. C, has been gaining.

76
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.818
The answer is B: gained.

77
00:05:46.983 --> 00:05:50.306
Unfair use of people or resources for profit is blank.

78
00:05:50.606 --> 00:05:54.794
A, exploitation. B, independence. C, sovereignty.

79
00:05:58.333 --> 00:06:00.797
The answer is A: exploitation.

80
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:03.868
Which collocation is correct?

81
00:06:04.168 --> 00:06:08.913
A, gain independence. B, win up independence. C, make independence.

82
00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:15.116
The answer is A: gain independence.

83
00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.258
According to the reading, many colonial institutions were designed to…

84
00:06:20.558 --> 00:06:25.895
A, serve citizens. B, extract resources. C, protect indigenous peoples.

85
00:06:29.433 --> 00:06:32.338
The answer is B: extract resources.

86
00:06:33.533 --> 00:06:36.522
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.383 --> 00:06:41.240
What lasting legacy of colonialism do you see in the world today?

88
00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.423
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:59.723 --> 00:07:36.888
One lasting legacy of colonialism I see is the shape of national borders, which were often drawn by colonial powers without regard for local communities. In Southeast Asia, several border disputes have continued for decades because of maps created under French or British rule. Colonialism has also influenced language; French words such as 'kafe' and 'pang' are still used in Khmer. Some people argue that colonial infrastructure brought benefits. Even so, I believe its costs for local people were far greater.

90
00:07:37.188 --> 00:07:39.060
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:44.727
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and present perfect for lasting effects.

92
00:07:45.027 --> 00:07:49.757
Today's value is historical awareness. Know the past so you can understand the present.

93
00:07:50.057 --> 00:07:52.501
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:52.801 --> 00:07:55.209
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

